\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Why flat minima generalize better. The validation loss landscape (dashed) is slightly shifted from training (solid). At a sharp minimum, this shift causes a large generalization gap. At a flat minimum, the same shift produces a much smaller gap---the solution is robust to distribution shift.}}{6}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flat_minima}{{5}{6}{Why flat minima generalize better. The validation loss landscape (dashed) is slightly shifted from training (solid). At a sharp minimum, this shift causes a large generalization gap. At a flat minimum, the same shift produces a much smaller gap---the solution is robust to distribution shift}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Autoencoder architecture. The encoder $f_\theta $ compresses high-dimensional input to a low-dimensional latent representation $z$. The decoder $g_\phi $ reconstructs the input from $z$. The bottleneck forces the network to learn which information matters.}}{6}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:autoencoder}{{6}{6}{Autoencoder architecture. The encoder $f_\theta $ compresses high-dimensional input to a low-dimensional latent representation $z$. The decoder $g_\phi $ reconstructs the input from $z$. The bottleneck forces the network to learn which information matters}{figure.6}{}}
